00:15:06,840 --> 00:15:12,784
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a neutral state, with indifference of the compulsion.

184
00:15:13,524 --> 00:15:13,845
Yeah.

185
00:15:16,903 --> 00:15:23,429
[SPEAKER_00]: But that neither... well, nothing's felt to the extent of the body.

186
00:15:24,470 --> 00:15:25,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly to the extent of the body.

187
00:15:27,372 --> 00:15:28,433
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing felt there.

188
00:15:28,934 --> 00:15:32,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Neither pleasure, nor displeasure is felt.

189
00:15:32,477 --> 00:15:32,938
[SPEAKER_00]: But is that so?

190
00:15:32,958 --> 00:15:35,119
[SPEAKER_01]: Wouldn't you say that neither pleasure nor pain is a feeling?

191
00:15:35,360 --> 00:15:35,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

192
00:15:35,960 --> 00:15:37,061
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not felt.

193
00:15:37,261 --> 00:15:38,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

194
00:15:38,082 --> 00:15:38,703
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it is.

195
00:15:39,907 --> 00:15:43,269
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why if you know neither pleasure nor non-pleasure, it's pleasant.

196
00:15:43,429 --> 00:15:44,869
[SPEAKER_00]: You know what not feeling is.

197
00:15:45,110 --> 00:15:45,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

198
00:15:45,710 --> 00:15:49,492
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't know what neither pleasure nor displeasure is, you are affected by it.

199
00:15:49,712 --> 00:15:52,413
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't know it, which means it's felt unpleasantly.

200
00:15:54,194 --> 00:16:01,137
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, even in case of an arahant, it's not that they discover some super extra dimension in between the feelings, so they don't feel.

201
00:16:01,197 --> 00:16:06,960
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's either pleasure, displeasure or neutral feeling, which is for them all neutral.

202
00:16:08,207 --> 00:16:09,267
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they don't feel any of it.

203
00:16:09,767 --> 00:16:12,208
[SPEAKER_00]: And because they know it, that's why it's pleasant.

204
00:16:12,708 --> 00:16:13,588
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Sariputra meant.

205
00:16:13,608 --> 00:16:13,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

206
00:16:13,808 --> 00:16:16,569
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact there's nothing felt, that's why it's pleasant.

207
00:16:17,809 --> 00:16:19,209
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on one level, it's nothing felt.

208
00:16:19,249 --> 00:16:20,609
[SPEAKER_00]: On another level, that is pleasure.

209
00:16:21,170 --> 00:16:24,050
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's a different type of pleasure.

210
00:16:24,090 --> 00:16:25,510
[SPEAKER_00]: So, four jhānas, it's exactly that.

211
00:16:26,151 --> 00:16:34,172
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you were to to develop the four jhāna, it would be impossible to not be another hunt.

212
00:16:36,153 --> 00:16:37,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Because four jhāna is not about random

213
00:16:40,159 --> 00:16:42,700
[SPEAKER_00]: magical, pleasurable attainment.

214
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:44,901
[SPEAKER_00]: You see the amount of clarity there.

215
00:16:45,061 --> 00:16:47,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Clarity about the domain of the hindrances.

216
00:16:47,522 --> 00:16:49,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Clarity about the extent of the body.

217
00:16:49,543 --> 00:16:51,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Clarity about the nature of the feeling.

218
00:16:51,784 --> 00:16:59,146
[SPEAKER_00]: Clarity about the unshakable, imperturbable context of mindfulness, whatever you want to translate it.

219
00:17:00,367 --> 00:17:00,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

220
00:17:00,587 --> 00:17:00,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

221
00:17:01,087 --> 00:17:01,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Being able...

222
00:17:01,447 --> 00:17:03,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Complete equanimity will have to be the result of it.

223
00:17:03,548 --> 00:17:04,989
[SPEAKER_01]: Being skilled in

224
00:17:06,088 --> 00:17:19,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, yeah, not, not feeling or like, like, like being unmoved by pleasant feeling or pleasant feeling.

225
00:17:19,933 --> 00:17:22,494
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, not as a matter of choice, but as a matter of development.

226
00:17:23,675 --> 00:17:26,876
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you want to be moved, it would be impossible to move.

227
00:17:27,016 --> 00:17:28,857
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just impossible.

228
00:17:33,482 --> 00:17:34,843
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yeah, that's the simile that you...

229
00:17:35,184 --> 00:17:35,544
[SPEAKER_01]: The white cloth.

230
00:17:35,564 --> 00:17:36,645
[SPEAKER_01]: You've already said the white cloth.

231
00:17:39,087 --> 00:17:39,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

232
00:17:39,327 --> 00:17:40,788
[SPEAKER_00]: So, exactly to the extent...

233
00:17:40,809 --> 00:17:41,289
[SPEAKER_01]: He says...

234
00:17:41,329 --> 00:17:42,670
[SPEAKER_01]: It's funny, he says he's sitting down.

235
00:17:44,432 --> 00:17:50,777
[SPEAKER_01]: He's sitting down, having pervaded this very body with a purified, clarified mind, so that there isn't any part of the body...

236
00:17:50,797 --> 00:17:51,678
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he's not...

237
00:17:51,938 --> 00:17:54,801
[SPEAKER_01]: The pervading isn't with a pleasure or joy or anything.

238
00:17:54,881 --> 00:17:55,521
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

239
00:17:55,822 --> 00:17:59,065
[SPEAKER_01]: It's pervading with a purified, clarified mind.

240
00:18:00,206 --> 00:18:00,606
[SPEAKER_01]: Imagine...

241
00:18:01,710 --> 00:18:02,851
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that case, you're not anymore.

242
00:18:03,111 --> 00:18:04,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Or in another, it's still the same.

243
00:18:27,310 --> 00:18:33,474
[SPEAKER_00]: The extent of the body gets pervaded by the clarity of the mind, which is now that perfect equanimity.

244
00:18:34,134 --> 00:18:52,345
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're pervading the extent of the body, from the tip of the head to the feet, by that pleasurable equanimity, pleasurable indifference, so to speak, of the completely unpolluted, fully developed compulsion.

245
00:18:54,386 --> 00:18:56,387
[SPEAKER_00]: But I'd like to answer that context, that kind of...

246
00:18:56,926 --> 00:19:06,839
[SPEAKER_00]: What has started indirectly, so to speak, you know, when you're pulled by hindrances, you need to keep discerning the background of the necessary basis for the hindrances, and then you get to approve them.

247
00:19:07,180 --> 00:19:13,088
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, now, going back to the level where the hindrances are, that's now indirect.

248
00:19:14,769 --> 00:19:20,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the mind got so established upon that peripheral, as the necessary base, as the, like, really what's first.

249
00:19:21,631 --> 00:19:23,391
[SPEAKER_00]: And that order cannot be perverted now.

250
00:19:24,351 --> 00:19:24,931
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

251
00:19:24,951 --> 00:19:31,033
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody can develop the mind to the extent of the four jhāna, it's virtually impossible to not be an ārahant then.

252
00:19:32,293 --> 00:19:33,473
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there will be nothing left.

253
00:19:34,814 --> 00:19:39,255
[SPEAKER_00]: They will not be included in the clarity of the right order, right context, nibbata, mindfulness.

254
00:19:41,543 --> 00:19:50,826
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all, any, all and any basis of passion, aversion and distraction are completely, sort of, evaporated.

255
00:19:58,068 --> 00:20:04,790
[SPEAKER_00]: But even without going all the way to four jhānas, it's the same principle and it's useful, I think, for people, because, you know, everybody likes the idea of jhānas.

256
00:20:04,890 --> 00:20:05,670
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing wrong with that.

257
00:20:07,611 --> 00:20:10,494
[SPEAKER_00]: But you want to understand that the pleasure has nothing to do with you.

258
00:20:12,196 --> 00:20:18,903
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you have a pleasure of your meditation, and if you can attend to it directly, that's not the pleasure of jhāna.

259
00:20:21,523 --> 00:20:25,104
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the pleasure of some novelty, of some sort of release or whatever you've been doing.

260
00:20:25,344 --> 00:20:28,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what everyone is going to experience when they first start meditation.

261
00:20:28,386 --> 00:20:29,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly, exactly.

262
00:20:29,486 --> 00:20:32,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you start calming down, you're going to have, like, as I said, like a novelty.

263
00:20:32,807 --> 00:20:33,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Wow!

264
00:20:33,147 --> 00:20:34,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

265
00:20:34,368 --> 00:20:43,331
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact that, see, you can relate to it directly, means it's not the result of your withdrawal from anything you can relate to directly.

266
00:20:45,196 --> 00:20:48,962
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the pleasure of the jhāna is... well, first of all, it's not pleasure of jhāna, it's pleasure of withdrawal.

267
00:20:49,142 --> 00:21:00,879
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you have absolutely no idea what seclusion from centrality and five hindrances is, you can be safe, then, to assume that whatever pleasure you feel in your meditation is not the pleasure you should be pursuing.

268
00:21:01,872 --> 00:21:02,812
[SPEAKER_00]: If it comes, fine.

269
00:21:02,953 --> 00:21:03,913
[SPEAKER_00]: If it goes, it's fine.

270
00:21:04,293 --> 00:21:06,434
[SPEAKER_00]: But don't think there's anything special about it.

271
00:21:06,954 --> 00:21:08,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't think it's about cultivating that.

272
00:21:08,695 --> 00:21:09,476
[SPEAKER_00]: That should be your job.

273
00:21:09,496 --> 00:21:10,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, exactly, exactly.

274
00:21:10,776 --> 00:21:15,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's not even about cultivating the wholesome pleasure of the withdrawal.

275
00:21:15,418 --> 00:21:20,621
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just using it as a symptom to, like, pervade that withdrawal further and further and further.

276
00:21:21,161 --> 00:21:23,422
[SPEAKER_01]: So, actually, really, it's funny talking about it now.

277
00:21:24,062 --> 00:21:25,603
[SPEAKER_01]: The body seems to be, like,

278
00:21:28,551 --> 00:21:31,673
[SPEAKER_01]: It's developing your mind in regard to the extent of the body.

279
00:21:31,693 --> 00:21:36,296
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's actually... that's exactly the reason why even an ārya is not an anāgāmī yet.

280
00:21:48,377 --> 00:21:51,759
[SPEAKER_00]: He is not developed to the... How did the sutra put it?

281
00:21:53,460 --> 00:21:55,642
[SPEAKER_00]: His mind is not developed in regard to the body, I think.

282
00:21:55,942 --> 00:21:56,502
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how it's said.

283
00:21:57,783 --> 00:21:59,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Or he hasn't been developed in the body.

284
00:22:01,165 --> 00:22:03,967
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why sensuality still arises for him.

285
00:22:06,849 --> 00:22:07,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Either the two types.

286
00:22:07,589 --> 00:22:10,871
[SPEAKER_00]: He hasn't been developed in regard to the body and he hasn't been developed in regard to the mind.

287
00:22:13,493 --> 00:22:14,954
[SPEAKER_00]: So he still has work to do.

288
00:22:15,912 --> 00:22:26,196
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have been developed in regard to the extent of your own body, regardless of the occasion, you have basically developed in regard to the extent of the hindrances, which means you cannot be hindered by them.

289
00:22:26,996 --> 00:22:31,137
[SPEAKER_00]: Because what they hinder, you're outside of it now, as in you have surmounted it.

290
00:22:32,538 --> 00:22:35,559
[SPEAKER_00]: By not surmounting it, their hindering hinders you.

291
00:22:35,639 --> 00:22:36,499
[SPEAKER_00]: You get hindered by it.

292
00:22:41,781 --> 00:22:44,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Some of the hindrances are also fetters, aren't they?

293
00:22:46,145 --> 00:22:47,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, yeah, but that's how it's different, though.

294
00:22:48,026 --> 00:22:54,929
[SPEAKER_00]: I was thinking, if... You're constantly hindered by something, then you end up developing a view over it, and that's when it's a fetter, like a fetter of doubt.

295
00:22:56,189 --> 00:22:57,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, yeah, okay, okay.

296
00:22:57,030 --> 00:23:10,616
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can free yourself from a fetter of doubt, as in understand the nature of doubt, but still be affected by doubt on a bodily level, in terms of basically feeling, craving in regard to the unpleasant feeling of doubting something.

297
00:23:11,576 --> 00:23:12,717
[SPEAKER_00]: That is a hindrance of doubt.

298
00:23:13,257 --> 00:23:14,878
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you've developed jhāna,

299
00:23:15,916 --> 00:23:16,977
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, even that cannot touch you.

300
00:23:17,177 --> 00:23:17,377
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

301
00:23:18,578 --> 00:23:20,759
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why arahant is the destroyer of the hindrances.

302
00:23:23,461 --> 00:23:27,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether he's in jhāna or not, those things cannot hinder him anymore, because his mind has developed above.

303
00:23:28,404 --> 00:23:30,405
[SPEAKER_00]: Even at its lowest level, it's still above.

304
00:23:31,826 --> 00:23:39,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Even at its least composed level, least recollected level, it's still above the five hindrances, which is exactly what what makes you an arahant.

305
00:23:41,532 --> 00:23:43,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Like when these fetters fall off, they cannot reappear.

306
00:23:44,113 --> 00:23:45,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Means you can't go below that.

307
00:24:02,785 --> 00:24:15,149
[SPEAKER_00]: So, peripheral awareness of the body is the starting point of the body that's enduring while you're talking, sitting, being silent, eating, going to the toilet.

308
00:24:15,809 --> 00:24:18,170
[SPEAKER_00]: The image of the body that is there is your starting point.

309
00:24:18,610 --> 00:24:19,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, actually, no.

310
00:24:19,210 --> 00:24:20,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Starting point is sense restraint.

311
00:24:20,851 --> 00:24:21,111
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

312
00:24:22,671 --> 00:24:30,254
[SPEAKER_01]: But the image of the body... and you're sort of getting at that via hindrances.

313
00:24:31,757 --> 00:24:32,257
[SPEAKER_00]: At the beginning?

314
00:24:32,317 --> 00:24:32,998
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way, yeah.

315
00:24:33,858 --> 00:24:35,319
[SPEAKER_00]: Having to endure hindrances as well.

316
00:24:35,539 --> 00:24:45,884
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, this sort of indirect pleasure that comes as a sort of... as you realize you're free from that, that then becomes the... the way.

317
00:24:47,444 --> 00:24:47,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

318
00:24:47,645 --> 00:24:55,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, not just you're free from that, but you realize it by... say, by establishing, by recollecting the extent of the body correctly, and then maintaining that recollectedness.

319
00:24:58,031 --> 00:25:03,112
[SPEAKER_00]: You immediately imply not just freedom from these hindrances, but freedom from the future hindrances.

320
00:25:03,232 --> 00:25:03,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

321
00:25:04,032 --> 00:25:09,514
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the whole domain, the possible things that could happen, none of it can apply and that is felt.

322
00:25:10,474 --> 00:25:12,014
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's such a tremendous relief.

323
00:25:13,495 --> 00:25:15,235
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's something you need to, like, fabricate.

324
00:25:15,255 --> 00:25:18,776
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to maintain the context that relief will have to come up with.

325
00:25:19,396 --> 00:25:23,737
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you can know, yes, you can't have a... you can have a relief without the jhāna.

326
00:25:25,776 --> 00:25:27,957
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't have a jhāna without a relief.

327
00:25:28,658 --> 00:25:30,318
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not because jhāna is the relief.

328
00:25:30,758 --> 00:25:31,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Jhāna is after the relief.

329
00:25:32,239 --> 00:25:39,222
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you get the relief, you get the pleasure, then you still keep doing the same thing, maintaining the same context.

330
00:25:40,096 --> 00:25:47,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Until you kind of become established upon it, so to speak, where it's not flimsy, where it doesn't go away as soon as you sort of... something pops up.

331
00:25:48,364 --> 00:25:50,866
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Buddha said steady your mind in this practice.

332
00:25:50,946 --> 00:25:52,908
[SPEAKER_00]: Steady your mind in the theme of jhāna.

333
00:25:53,509 --> 00:25:55,531
[SPEAKER_00]: So you can do that even before jhāna.

334
00:25:55,591 --> 00:25:56,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, he dwells.

335
00:25:57,072 --> 00:25:57,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

336
00:25:57,752 --> 00:26:05,136
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you steady your mind, then you actually dwell in that steadied mind of the relief from everything unwholesome and concerns about the world.

337
00:26:05,576 --> 00:26:06,577
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what jhāna is.

338
00:26:06,597 --> 00:26:07,537
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you dwell in it.

339
00:26:07,777 --> 00:26:09,418
[SPEAKER_00]: The reality could just be living.

340
00:26:09,598 --> 00:26:10,879
[SPEAKER_00]: He lives.

341
00:26:10,899 --> 00:26:12,079
[SPEAKER_00]: He lives in that state.

342
00:26:13,080 --> 00:26:17,442
[SPEAKER_00]: And when I say state, I mean that establishment, not like some sort of oblivious, oblivion.

343
00:26:17,462 --> 00:26:17,922
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

344
00:26:18,482 --> 00:26:18,822
[SPEAKER_00]: He lives.

345
00:26:18,882 --> 00:26:19,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

346
00:26:19,142 --> 00:26:26,506
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha says, whether he walks, talks, goes to toilet, eats, he's in... whatever he does is divine because he's in jhāna.

347
00:26:34,016 --> 00:26:44,604
[SPEAKER_00]: So, practicing the recollectiveness of the extent of your body, or, say, mindfulness of the bodily postures, regardless of what hindrance is there...

348
00:26:46,005 --> 00:26:53,031
[SPEAKER_00]: So, which means, you're not acting out of hindrance, and you're then maintaining the context, despite the pressure of the hindrances.

349
00:26:53,051 --> 00:26:54,472
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you go beyond the hindrances.

350
00:26:55,582 --> 00:27:01,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, sometimes it will be more pleasurable, sometimes it will be less, depending on how many hindrances you have arising, how often.

351
00:27:02,587 --> 00:27:03,888
[SPEAKER_00]: And that depends on you, really.

352
00:27:04,889 --> 00:27:06,550
[SPEAKER_01]: How much you've been giving in to them before.

353
00:27:09,993 --> 00:27:16,117
[SPEAKER_00]: But the less you act out of them, the less you give in to them, the less you try to get rid of them, the less you try to indulge them, the less they will arise.

354
00:27:16,177 --> 00:27:19,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, then, your pleasure of jhāna will be more and more agreeable.

355
00:27:19,840 --> 00:27:21,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Your practice of jhāna will be more and more agreeable.

356
00:27:22,592 --> 00:27:29,956
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you're, if you're, if you are acting out of the hindrance, you're basically...

357
00:27:29,976 --> 00:27:36,319
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way to act out of hindrance, I know what you... yeah, the only way you act out of hindrance is to lose the extent of the body.

358
00:27:36,339 --> 00:27:38,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly, because you're, you're, you're putting yourself first.

359
00:27:38,801 --> 00:27:40,441
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I, I, I can control...

360
00:27:40,562 --> 00:27:43,203
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you catch yourself acting out of hindrance, it's not like, oh, I...

361
00:27:43,523 --> 00:27:44,424
[SPEAKER_00]: Next time I won't do it.

362
00:27:44,484 --> 00:27:45,064
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you will.

363
00:27:45,385 --> 00:27:45,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

364
00:27:45,825 --> 00:27:55,953
[SPEAKER_00]: What you want to do is, when you catch yourself acting out of hindrance, now include all of that in the extent of the body, and that's how next time you will not act as much, at least, out of the hindrance.

365
00:27:58,596 --> 00:28:03,059
[SPEAKER_01]: Until it becomes possible to realize, actually, it's not me who is in control.

366
00:28:03,079 --> 00:28:05,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, until the clarity becomes apparent.

367
00:28:05,260 --> 00:28:06,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, hey, wait a minute.

